<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="sso"
      updated="August 23, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"sso-login", "Setting up SSO login"},
        {"signing-in", "How members sign in"},
        {"operations", "Managing a connection"},
        {"providers", "Provider guides"},
        {"generic-oidc", "Any other OIDC provider"},
        {"troubleshooting", "Troubleshooting"},
        {"security-notes", "Security notes"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="sso">
        <:dek>
          SSO allows you to sign in to emisar through your own identity provider — Google
          Workspace, Okta, Microsoft Entra, JumpCloud, Keycloak, or any compliant OIDC
          provider.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Provisioning and removal from your directory is a separate layer on top of sign-in: <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/scim"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >directory sync (SCIM)</.link>.
        Both are managed from <a
          href={~p"/app/sso"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-400 underline decoration-zinc-700 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Team → Single sign-on</a>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="sso-login">Setting up SSO login</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        You need an emisar owner or admin, and enough access at your provider to create an
        application. <.plan_note tier={:team} />
      </p>
      <ol class="mt-5 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-base text-zinc-400">
        <li>
          Open
          <a
            href={~p"/app/sso/new"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-400 underline decoration-zinc-700 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-300"
          >Team → Single sign-on → Add provider</a>
          and choose your provider type. Leave the page open — it shows the redirect URI your
          provider asks for next.
        </li>
        <li>
          At the provider, create a
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">confidential web application</strong>
          — one with a client secret. Paste emisar's redirect URI into it unchanged; it ends in <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/sign_in/sso/callback</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Back in emisar, enter the issuer URL, client ID, and client secret. Press <strong class="text-zinc-200">Test connection</strong>, then tick
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Enabled</strong>
          and save — otherwise sign-ins will not work.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The <a href="#providers" class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">provider guides</a>
        below walk each of these screens with screenshots.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="connection-fields">Connection settings</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The <strong class="text-zinc-200">provider type</strong>
        selects a preset that chooses the identity claim and provider-specific behavior —
        pick your actual provider rather than Generic OIDC. The
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">display name</strong>
        is shown on the sign-in page.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        emisar fetches the <strong class="text-zinc-200">issuer URL</strong>'s OIDC discovery
        document and derives every other endpoint from it. Only enter the issuer. Do not enter
        authorization, token, or JWKS URLs. It must use HTTPS and be reachable from emisar.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The <strong class="text-zinc-200">client ID</strong>
        and <strong class="text-zinc-200">client secret</strong>
        come from the application. The secret is write-only: emisar stores it server-side and
        never shows it again — it must replay the secret on every sign-in, so it cannot store
        only a hash. Leave the field blank when editing later to keep the current secret.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The <strong class="text-zinc-200">identifier claim</strong>
        is the field emisar stores as a person's permanent id:
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>
        for most providers, <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">oid</code>
        for Microsoft Entra. Leave it unchanged — <a
          href="#subject-not-email"
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >emisar
        recognises a returning member by this value</a>, so it locks permanently at the first
        sign-in. Your directory must send the same value as SCIM
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/scim"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"><code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">externalId</code></.link>
        so sign-in and directory sync identify one person.
      </p>

      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/sso/sso-connection-fields.webp"
        title="emisar console · Add connection"
        caption="The identifier claim already defaults to the OIDC standard; only Microsoft Entra needs another."
        alt="The OIDC connection section of emisar's Add connection form: an Issuer URL field placeholdered 'your provider's OIDC issuer URL (the discovery base)' and noted as fetched for discovery over HTTPS, empty Client ID and Client secret fields side by side, and an Identifier claim select set to 'sub — OIDC standard'."
      />

      <.docs_h3 id="provisioning-fields">Who gets in, and what they get</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">New-user provisioning</strong>
        controls a first sign-in when no emisar member exists:
        <span class="text-zinc-300">Auto-provision</span>
        creates the member immediately, and <span class="text-zinc-300">Manual</span>
        holds the first sign-in for administrator approval. Auto-provision is the usual setup
        when the provider application is assigned only to approved users; use Manual when you
        cannot control assignments that tightly.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Turning on
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/scim"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">
          directory sync
        </.link>
        overrides this setting: the directory decides who exists, and a sign-in from an
        unprovisioned account waits for approval either way.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Default role for new users</strong>
        is configurable. Provisioning never grants <span class="text-zinc-300">owner</span>
        — that stays a deliberate human grant, so no provider misconfiguration can grant this
        role. If you map directory groups to roles, the mapping wins and this is only the
        fallback for someone in no mapped group. See
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/teams-and-access"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">roles</.link>
        for the list of roles and their permissions.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Default runner access</strong>
        controls which hosts a new member can see and use.
        <span class="text-zinc-300">No runners</span>
        is the safe default — the member reads the console and reaches nothing until you
        grant access. <span class="text-zinc-300">All runners</span>
        covers current and future runners, and <span class="text-zinc-300">Selected runners</span>
        limits access to named groups or hosts. Roles and runner access are independent: an
        operator with no runners cannot dispatch anything.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Allowed email domain</strong>
        is optional, and refuses a sign-in from outside one domain. It reads the provider's
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">hd</code>
        claim when the provider sends one, and otherwise the domain of the
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">email</code>
        claim — never the identifier claim, which says who someone is rather than where their
        mail lives. Either source counts only if the provider also sends <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">email_verified</code>, so a self-asserted address cannot pass. Leave the
        field blank to accept every address the provider returns.
      </p>

      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/sso/sso-provisioning-fields.webp"
        title="emisar console · Add connection"
        caption="The state every member provisioned through this connection starts in."
        alt="The Member provisioning section: a New member provisioning select set to 'Auto-provision new members on first sign-in', a Default role choice of admin, billing manager, operator, or viewer with viewer selected, a Default access choice of no runners (selected), all runners, or selected runners, and an optional Allowed email domain field."
        preview_h="max-h-[32rem]"
      />

      <.docs_h3 id="activation-fields">Satisfying the 2FA requirement</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Enable this setting if your provider satisfies the account's 2FA requirement. It saves
        members from setting up a second 2FA token for emisar. Only turn it on if the provider
        really enforces MFA — otherwise it becomes a way around the account's requirement,
        which is described in <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/authentication" <> "#enforcement"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Enforce
        account sign-in</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/docs/sso/sso-activation-fields.webp"
        title="emisar console · Add connection"
        caption="Both boxes start off, so a saved connection is inert until you enable it."
        alt="The Security and activation section: an unchecked box reading 'Sign-in through this provider satisfies the account's 2FA requirement', noted as safe only when the provider enforces MFA itself, and an unchecked 'Enabled (members can sign in through this connection)' box."
      />

      <.docs_h2 id="signing-in">How members sign in</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        On the sign-in page, members select
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">“Continue with single sign-on”</strong>
        (<code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/sign_in/sso</code>), enter the
        team's address, choose the provider, authenticate there, and return signed in.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="subject-not-email">When the email already belongs to someone</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        emisar recognises a returning member by the identifier claim, not by their email
        address. People change addresses, and an address can be reassigned; a subject cannot.
        Matching on email would let whoever controls an address inherit the emisar account
        behind it, so a first SSO sign-in never silently merges into an existing one. What
        happens instead depends on who owns the address:
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Already a member of this account.</strong>
            The sign-in is held, and an owner or admin approves it under <a
              href={~p"/app/team"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-400 underline decoration-zinc-700 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-300"
            >Team → Pending access requests</a>. Approving binds
            that existing member to the SSO identity — it does not create a second person, and
            their role and runner access are left as they are.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Someone outside this account.</strong>
            The sign-in is refused. There is nobody here to link them to, and emisar will not
            claim another account's user by matching an address.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="operations">Managing a connection</.docs_h2>
      <.docs_h3 id="require-sso">Require SSO for the account</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        First complete a real sign-in through the connection in another browser.
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Test connection</strong>
        proves discovery and reachability only — not user assignment, callback acceptance, or
        claims — so it can pass on a connection nobody can actually sign in through. Keep an
        owner session open while you test. Then open
        <a
          href={~p"/app/team"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-400 underline decoration-zinc-700 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Team → Security</a>
        and turn on <strong class="text-zinc-200">Require single sign-on</strong>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Once required, magic-link sign-in stops working, and existing sessions are redirected
        to one of this account's enabled providers on their next navigation. This option
        cannot be enabled with no active SSO connection, and emisar refuses to disable or
        delete the last active connection while the requirement remains on. Turn off Require
        SSO before retiring that last connection.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Require SSO gates browser access and new OAuth consent for every member, including
        owners. What it covers — and the bearer credentials it leaves untouched — is in <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/authentication" <> "#enforcement"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >what Require SSO covers</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="rotate-secret">Rotate a client secret</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The client secret is a credential like any other — the rotation steps, including the
        write-only field and the sign-in check before you delete the old secret, are in <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/credentials" <> "#idp"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Rotate and revoke credentials</.link>. If you suspect a leak,
        follow
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/security-incidents" <> "#idp"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Security incidents</.link>
        instead.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="move-provider">Move to another issuer or client</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        After an SSO connection's first sign-in, its issuer, client ID, and identifier claim
        cannot be updated any more. To move tenants, issuers, or clients, add a new connection
        and migrate assignments to it. You can still change the client secret to rotate it.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="disable-provider">Disable or delete a connection</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Disabling a connection stops new sign-ins but keeps the configuration, so you can
        enable it again later. Deleting retires it and dismisses any manual-approval requests
        still waiting on it. Either way, the sessions that connection signed in are revoked,
        and users and audit history are kept.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        An account can have several provider types, but only one enabled connection of each type.
        Only one enabled emisar connection can use a given email domain. Remove or disable the
        existing domain before assigning it elsewhere.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="providers">Provider guides</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each guide walks one provider end to end — sign-in first, then directory sync — with
        screenshots of the console screens along the way.
      </p>

      <ul class="mt-5 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li id="okta" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/okta"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Okta</.link>
            — an OIDC web app for sign-in and a separate SCIM app for the directory.
            Verified against a live Okta Integrator org on August 18, 2026.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li id="entra" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/entra"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Microsoft Entra</.link>
            — an app registration with the
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">oid</code>
            claim, and an enterprise application for provisioning.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li id="jumpcloud" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/jumpcloud"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >JumpCloud</.link>
            — one custom application carries sign-in and provisioning together.
            Verified against a live JumpCloud tenant on August 18, 2026.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li id="keycloak" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/keycloak"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Keycloak</.link>
            uses a confidential client with PKCE and has no outbound SCIM.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li id="google-workspace" class="flex gap-3">
          <span class="mt-2.5 h-1 w-1 flex-none rounded-full bg-zinc-600"></span>
          <span>
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/integrations/google-workspace"}
              class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Google Workspace</.link>
            uses an Internal OAuth client with a locked, prefilled issuer
            and no outbound SCIM.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="generic-oidc">Any other OIDC provider</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The preset providers are built on top of one standard flow — they are not separate
        integrations.
        A provider that meets the contract below works through
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Generic OpenID Connect</strong>
        — including Auth0, Ping, OneLogin, Authentik, or an in-house IdP. This connection uses only
        the OIDC authorization code flow. SAML is not supported.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The <a href="#sso-login" class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">setup steps</a>
        above are the same here. Two extra requirements apply to a provider with no preset: its
        issuer must serve <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/.well-known/openid-configuration</code>, and it must
        advertise <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">client_secret_post</code>
        or <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">client_secret_basic</code>
        for client authentication. The
        <a href="#oidc-contract" class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">contract
        below</a>
        is the full list emisar checks.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="oidc-contract">OIDC contract</.docs_h3>
      <div class="mt-5 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-900/60 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Part</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">What emisar requires</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-300">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Discovery</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                The issuer must use HTTPS and have a valid OpenID Provider Configuration. Its
                authorization, token, and JWKS endpoints must also use public HTTPS. Private,
                loopback, and cloud-metadata targets are refused.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Authorization</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                The provider must support authorization code flow with PKCE. The system uses S256
                when the provider advertises it. It accepts plain only when the provider advertises
                plain as its PKCE method. The request includes <code>openid</code>, <code>email</code>, <code>profile</code>, <code>state</code>, and <code>nonce</code>.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Client authentication</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                A confidential client using <code>client_secret_post</code>
                or <code>client_secret_basic</code>. Public clients and signing-key client
                authentication are not supported.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">ID-token claims</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                The ID token must contain a stable <code>sub</code>
                and an <code>email</code>
                marked by <code>email_verified: true</code>. It can also contain <code>name</code>. emisar reads
                these claims from the ID token and does not call UserInfo. The Entra preset uses
                <code>oid</code>
                instead of <code>sub</code>.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Validation</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                The callback must pass state, nonce, PKCE, ID-token signature, exact issuer,
                audience, and expiration checks before emisar resolves an identity.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <.docs_h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Use these checks for common sign-in symptoms. If the symptom is somewhere else in the
        product, start from <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/troubleshooting"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >troubleshooting</.link>, which routes each one to its owner page.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-5 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-900/60 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Symptom</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Check</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-300">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                Test connection cannot load discovery
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Use the issuer, not an authorization or token URL. Confirm it serves
                <code>/.well-known/openid-configuration</code>
                over public HTTPS and does not
                publish private or non-HTTPS endpoints.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                The IdP rejects the callback or token exchange
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Compare the redirect URI byte for byte, confirm the app is a confidential web
                client, and replace an expired client secret. Turn off DPoP if the provider
                offers it — a sender-constrained token breaks the token request. For a generic
                provider, confirm a PKCE method and one of the two supported secret-auth
                methods are advertised.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                Authentication succeeds but emisar refuses the identity
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Inspect the ID token. It must contain the configured stable identifier and a
                verified email. UserInfo-only email or profile claims are not read. If an existing
                emisar user has that email, an administrator must link the provider identity
                deliberately. emisar never merges by email.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                Members loop back to SSO after Require SSO
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Use the owner session you kept open to turn off Require SSO, then complete a real
                provider sign-in before enabling it again. If no owner session remains, contact <a
                  href={EmisarWeb.MailTo.support()}
                  class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
                >support</a>.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">
                SSO and SCIM create different identities
              </td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Make SCIM <code>externalId</code>
                equal the OIDC identifier claim — the convergence contract and per-provider
                claims are in <.link
                  navigate={~p"/docs/scim" <> "#troubleshooting"}
                  class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
                >Directory sync</.link>.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <.docs_h2 id="security-notes">Security notes</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Every SSO sign-in lands in the
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/audit-and-siem"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >audit log</.link>
        with its authentication method.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
